<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diaguita</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Diaguitas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diaguita were a group of indigenous peoples speaking the Cacán language who lived in the valleys of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and north-central Chile. Skilled farmers, potters and metalworkers, they came partly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule in the fifteenth century and fiercely resisted the Spanish in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchaqui-wars">Calchaquí Wars</a> until the 1660s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diaguita.jpg" alt="Diaguita" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Diaguita_people_in_Chile.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
